    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Since 2005’s Kelo case, the interpretation of the Fifth Amendment with regard to eminent domain takings of private real property has become a subject of fierce public debate. The Fifth Amendment requires that any properties taken by the government must be A) for the public use, and B) justly compensated. Because the Kelo decision greatly expanded the definition of public use, defining it more as public purpose or public benefit, controversy has mostly focused on the public use clause. This article argues that the just-compensation requirement offers fruitful possibilities for re-evaluation; specifically, that state governments could better protect property rights by mandating above-market compensation, rather than market-rate compensation, for eminent domain takings. Such a requirement, as has been partially enacted in Michigan, could better balance the needs of the government and the individual, especially in situations where adverse incentives otherwise exist.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
